{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Combinatorics.SimpleGraph.DegreeSum","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.bond_sum_lt_pair_target","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.bond_sum_lt_pair_target","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BoundedDegreeCoulomb.lean#L111-L121","doc":"Restated on the graph itself: past `N`, a degree-bounded bond sum is below\nthe Coulomb target for that same vertex set. This is the form the measurement\ninstantiates — `d = 2` for the chain, `d = 5` for the max-contact deltahedra,\n`d = 12` for close packing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.exists_gt_bounded_degree_bond_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.exists_gt_bounded_degree_bond_sum","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BoundedDegreeCoulomb.lean#L84-L109","doc":"**No constant per-bond energy makes a degree-bounded bond sum quadratic.**\n\nGiven any per-bond energy `ε ≥ 0`, any degree bound `d ≥ 0` and any Coulomb\ncoefficient `c > 0`, past a computable size EVERY graph with that degree bound\nhas `ε · (its bond count)` strictly below `c · (its pair count)`. The bond sum is\nlinear; the pair count is not; and no choice of the letters closes the gap.\n\nCompare `BindingExtensivity.exists_pow_gt_linear`, which separates a different\npair of growth classes (exponential vs linear) for a different purpose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.edge_share_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.edge_share_le","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BoundedDegreeCoulomb.lean#L73-L82","doc":"The fraction of PAIRS that a degree-bounded graph's edges cover is at most\n`d/(n-1)`. At `d = 2` (a chain) this is the measured `2/(Z-1)`; at `d = 12`\n(close packing) it is `12/(Z-1)`. Bounded either way, and vanishing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.two_mul_card_edge_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.BoundedDegreeCoulomb.two_mul_card_edge_le","kind":"theorem","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/BoundedDegreeCoulomb.lean#L62-L71","doc":"**The degree-sum formula as a bond-count bound.**\nA graph in which no vertex has more than `d` neighbours has at most `dn/2`\nedges, whatever else is true of it. This is the whole content of \"a chain cannot\ncarry Coulomb\", with the chain replaced by its only relevant property. ","signatureChunk":0}]}